[
  {
    "id": "p-8f4bb1",
    "offset": 0,
    "text": "1. Give, as far as possible, the logical converse of each of the following propositions--"
  },
  {
    "id": "p-603d8b",
    "offset": 91,
    "text": "(1) Energy commands success."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-0638ee",
    "offset": 121,
    "text": "(2) Mortals cannot be happy."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-64a715",
    "offset": 151,
    "text": "(3) There are mistakes which are criminal."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-0ce0ce",
    "offset": 195,
    "text": "(4) All's well that ends well."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-f48ca2",
    "offset": 227,
    "text": "(5) Envious men are disliked."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-d10c63",
    "offset": 258,
    "text": "(6) A term is a kind of word or collection of words."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-1dedbc",
    "offset": 312,
    "text": "(7) Some Frenchmen are not vivacious."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-8a0189",
    "offset": 351,
    "text": "(8) All things in heaven and earth were hateful to him."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-e5ed18",
    "offset": 408,
    "text": "(9) The square of three is nine."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-51c17e",
    "offset": 442,
    "text": "(10) All cannot receive this saying."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-dc8f81",
    "offset": 480,
    "text": "(11) P struck Q."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-9a79ba",
    "offset": 498,
    "text": "(12) Amas."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-59b031",
    "offset": 510,
    "text": "2. 'More things may be contained in my philosophy than exist in heaven or earth: but the converse proposition is by no means true.' Is the term converse here used in its logical meaning?"
  }
]